<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deccan Plateau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> Plateau is the elevated tableland covering much of central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its rugged, fortified uplands and semi-arid agriculture formed the geographic setting in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> emerged, providing defensible strongholds and a base for the Maratha economy and expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau.jpg" alt="Deccan Plateau" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dek%C3%A1nsk%C3%A1_krajina_u_Hajdar%C3%A1b%C3%A1du.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Deccan Plateau extends over about 422,000 square kilometres in the western, central and southern regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, forming the bulk of the Deccan Peninsula. It stretches from the Satpura and Vindhya ranges in the north to the northern fringes of the Nilgiri Hills in the south and is bounded by the Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a>. It covers most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> excluding their coastal regions. The terrain is rocky, with an average elevation of about 600 metres, and the plateau is subdivided into the Maharashtra, Karnataka and Telangana plateaus.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> block rain-bearing winds, so the plateau is drier than the coast and has a semi-arid climate. Rivers such as the Godavari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Kaveri flow eastwards to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. In the north-west the Deccan Traps are layers of basalt more than 2 kilometres thick, laid down by lava flows at the end of the Cretaceous period about 66 million years ago. The word Deccan comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> dakṣiṇa, meaning south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ash mounds associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> cultures date back to 8000 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">iron technology</a> spread through the region towards 1000 BCE. Since at least the 1st century BCE the region has been connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> and to trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Dynasties such as the Satavahanas, Pallavas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, Rashtrakutas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a>, Kakatiyas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> ruled parts of it between the 6th century BCE and the 14th century CE.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> held most of the southern part of the plateau, while the upper part was ruled by the Bahmani kingdom and its successors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, briefly captured the region in the early 18th century. After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> in the late 18th century and the Vellore Mutiny of 1806 the British East India Company consolidated its power, and the British took direct control in 1857. After independence in 1947 states were reorganised on linguistic lines in the 1950s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> was created in 2014.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The plateau is one of the oldest and most stable land formations of the subcontinent and is rich in iron ore, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and mica, and has yielded precious and semi-precious stones. Major cities on it include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bengaluru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. Historians have used the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> differently over time, and Stewart Gordon called it a relational term for the area beyond the southern border of a northern kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deccan Plateau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He soon moved his capital to Gulbarga, which he renamed Ahsanabad, in the heart of the <b>Deccan plateau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukya kingdom rested on the agriculture of the <b>Deccan plateau</b> and the valleys of the Krishna, Malaprabha and Tungabhadra rivers, where millets, pulses, cotton and, on irrigated land, rice were grown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maratha Empire arose on the <b>Deccan Plateau</b> in the mid-17th century as a regional power challenging the Deccan sultanates and, in time, the Mughal Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lukacs and Hemphill, from dental evidence, place the direct descendants of its Neolithic people to the south and east, in north-western India and on the western edge of the <b>Deccan Plateau</b>: Neolithic Mehrgarh shows a greater affinity with Chalcolithic Inamgaon than with Chalcolithic Mehrgarh itself.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mehrgarh-culture">Mehrgarh culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ajanta Caves lie in the northern wall of the U-shaped gorge of the River Waghur on the <b>Deccan Plateau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
